WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:05.840
Neurons are specialized cells that transmit&nbsp;
information. Within the nervous system, they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:11.040
are arranged into complex networks of circuits&nbsp;
that enable our thoughts, sensations, and actions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.920 --> 00:00:17.280
Neurons transmit information through two different&nbsp;
molecular processes. In the first of these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:22.720
processes, a signal is passed from cell to cell&nbsp;
via chemical messengers called neurotransmitters.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:30.320
This occurs at synapse, the convergence point of&nbsp;
two neurons. One neuron releases neurotransmitters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:43.840
from storage vesicles into the synaptic&nbsp;
cleft a small gap separating the two neurons.

00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:50.880
There are many types of neurotransmitters&nbsp;
that carry different types of signals.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:55.760
For example, the neurotransmitter&nbsp;
glutamate is an agent in pain signaling,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.760 --> 00:01:01.120
among many other pathways. As shown&nbsp;
here, glutamate binds to its receptor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:05.840
causing the receptor to open its ion&nbsp;
channel and allow ions to enter the neuron.

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:13.040
This ionic influx initiates the&nbsp;
second signaling process that neurons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:19.280
employ — the action potential. To understand&nbsp;
how these ions initiate the action potential,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:25.520
we need to examine the neuronal membrane when the&nbsp;
neuron is at rest. In the resting state, various&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.520 --> 00:01:31.280
types of ions surround the membrane. Outside&nbsp;
the neuron, the dominant ion type is sodium,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:36.320
while inside, the dominant ion type is&nbsp;
potassium — both are positively charged.

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:42.080
This unbalanced ionic distribution forms&nbsp;
concentration gradients across the membrane.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:47.840
Neurons establish and maintain these gradients&nbsp;
largely by the sodium-potassium pump,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.840 --> 00:01:51.600
a membrane protein that pumps&nbsp;
three sodium ions out of the neuron&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:54.320
for every two potassium ions that it pumps in.

00:01:56.880 --> 00:02:02.640
But there is one other kind of gradient here&nbsp;
— a gradient of charge. The positive charges&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:07.680
on the outside are balanced to some degree&nbsp;
by the chloride ions. On the inside, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:12.960
positive charges are balanced to a better degree&nbsp;
by the net negative charges of cellular proteins.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:19.200
This creates a positive charge on the outside&nbsp;
of the neuron relative to the inside. We call&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:25.440
this difference in charge across the membrane a&nbsp;
voltage. In neurons, voltage-gated ion channels&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:31.360
act as gatekeepers for ion traffic across&nbsp;
the membrane. Thanks to their voltage-sensing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.360 --> 00:02:36.320
domains, these channels open and close in&nbsp;
response to changes in membrane voltage.

00:02:40.960 --> 00:02:47.440
Remember that influx of ions we saw earlier? It&nbsp;
causes a change in the voltage! Voltage-gated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.440 --> 00:02:53.280
sodium channels sense this change and open,&nbsp;
allowing sodium ions into the neuron. These&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.280 --> 00:02:59.760
sodium ions are flowing down gradients of both&nbsp;
concentration and charge. Their influx triggers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:05.200
more sodium channels to open, creating a positive&nbsp;
feedback loop as neighboring sodium channels&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.200 --> 00:03:11.360
activate one another. This process of continuous&nbsp;
amplification is called an action potential.

00:03:12.320 --> 00:03:18.000
To prepare for the next action potential the&nbsp;
neuron must terminate the signal. This occurs in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:23.440
four steps. At a critical point the sodium&nbsp;
channels close and become temporarily&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.440 --> 00:03:29.920
inactivated blocking further sodium ion entry.&nbsp;
Meanwhile the voltage-gated potassium channels&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.920 --> 00:03:35.120
offset the change in voltage by opening and&nbsp;
allowing potassium ions to exit the neuron.

00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:46.400
Concurrently, the sodium-potassium pump&nbsp;
restores the original ionic gradients,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.400 --> 00:03:50.560
pumping sodium out and potassium&nbsp;
in in a three-to-two ratio.

00:03:51.280 --> 00:03:57.280
Finally, after a short refractory period, the&nbsp;
sodium channels reopen their inactivation gates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.280 --> 00:03:59.840
and become ready for the next action potential.

00:04:00.960 --> 00:04:04.160
Once the action potential&nbsp;
reaches the end of the axon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.160 --> 00:04:09.200
it stimulates neurotransmitter release&nbsp;
propagating the signal onto the next neuron.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.760 --> 00:04:15.280
Neurotransmitter release depends on the presence&nbsp;
of calcium ions which are abundant outside the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.280 --> 00:04:21.680
neuron but sparse inside. The action potential&nbsp;
triggers voltage-gated calcium channels to open&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.680 --> 00:04:27.440
allowing an influx of calcium ions. These&nbsp;
calcium ions then activate special proteins&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.440 --> 00:04:32.080
that mediate the fusing of vesicles with&nbsp;
the membrane. The vesicles release their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.080 --> 00:04:41.840
neurotransmitters into the synaptic cleft which&nbsp;
diffuse across and start the signaling cycle anew

00:04:43.040 --> 00:04:47.840
Together neurotransmitters,&nbsp;
gradients, ion channels, and ion pumps&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.840 --> 00:04:55.840
work in harmony to create the&nbsp;
neuronal signaling symphony.

